The Moon doesn’t merely symbolize change over the Pacific—it physically moves the ocean through tides. Those tides create repeating changes in depth, current, shoreline access, salinity, feeding opportunity, predator exposure, spawning conditions, and navigation. Organisms can synchronize behavior to that cycle.

Then humans observe the same cycle and turn it into tide tables, fishing schedules, sailing practice, calendars, metaphors, gods, stories, and predictions.

Same celestial pattern, multiple substrates:

Moon → gravity → Pacific tide → ecology → animal behavior → human observation → model → story → planned behavior.

And then comes your more interesting move: the story can loop back into the physical world. A fisherman who believes “incoming tide means feeding fish” changes when and where he fishes. Whether his explanation is perfectly complete or not, the explanation has become causal because he acts through it.

